# `for (let i = 0; i < point[key]; i++) take(i)`: `i` has no int32 written and is a parameter of the head of the loop; the relation that bounds it stands on the way into the body, where it enters the parameter of `take`.
# The promise is judged in the block in which it is made. Once it was judged in the block of the value, in front of which no relation stands: "it can be anything from 0 to 9007199254740992". Unseen for `i < point.x`: there the walk narrows by itself.
